# ===========================================================================
#  THIS FILE IS COMMITTED. Never put a real password in it.
#
#      cp server/.env.example server/.env
#
#  .env is git-ignored and is the only safe place for credentials. Editing
#  this file changes nothing: the app reads .env, not .env.example.
# ===========================================================================
#
# Everything here has a working default except the mail relay, so the app runs
# with this file untouched — it just logs emails instead of sending them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sending real email
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Without this block, invitations and verification links are written to the
# server log rather than delivered, and the invite toast tells you so.
#
# Gmail, which is what most people have to hand:
#
#   1. Turn on 2-Step Verification: https://myaccount.google.com/security
#      App passwords do not exist as an option until you do.
#   2. Create an app password: https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
#      Pick "Mail" / "Other", name it SyncSpace.
#   3. Paste the 16 characters below exactly as Google shows them. The spaces
#      are display only and are stripped for you.
#
# Then prove it works before trusting it with an invite:
#
#   cd server && npm run mail:check -- you@example.com
#
SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=sinchalkar001@gmail.com
SMTP_PASS=abcd efgh ijkl mnop

# The From header. Optional when SMTP_USER is itself an address, which is the
# Gmail case — a relay only accepts a From it recognises anyway.
# MAIL_FROM=SyncSpace <your.address@gmail.com>

# A provider that gives you one URL instead of four parts. Use this OR the
# SMTP_HOST block above, never both. Note that a password containing @ or :
# has to be percent-encoded here, which is the reason the parts exist.
# SMTP_URL=smtps://user:pass@mail.example.com:465

# Where emailed links point. Defaults to the first CORS_ORIGIN, which is the
# same app in every deployment we run. Set it if the client lives elsewhere.
# CLIENT_URL=http://localhost:5173

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything else
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PORT=4000
# MONGODB_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/syncspace

# 32+ characters. A development default is used when unset; production refuses
# to start without a real one.
# JWT_SECRET=

# Comma-separated absolute origins, no wildcards. Required in production.
# CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# Running a room's buffer executes a real program on this machine, unsandboxed.
# Worth switching off anywhere the people in a room are not people you trust.
# ALLOW_CODE_EXECUTION=true
